{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 64, :comments ["<b>And she saw Isaac</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 24:64): she saw him resplendent and was ashamed before him. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] explained that the latter verse is transposed, for 'and she said to the servant' — yet she had already said to the servant, and there are many such instances in the Torah according to his opinion. And in truth some such cases are indeed found, but here it is not necessary to transpose the two verses: 'And Rebekah lifted her eyes and saw Isaac, and she said to the servant, Who is that man walking in the field to meet us? And the servant said, He is my master, and she fell from the camel and took the veil and covered herself.' In my opinion, when Rebekah saw a man walking toward her in the field — that he had turned aside from the road and was walking in the field alongside them — she knew that he was coming to see them and to greet them in peace, or to bring them into his house to lodge, and she acted according to the way of women's modesty to stand with reserve." "<b>And she fell from the camel</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 24:64): she let herself down to the ground, as in its Aramaic translation וְאִתְרְכִינַת, like 'Tip your jar' (Genesis 24:14) — אַרְכִינִי, and 'He bowed the heavens and came down' (Psalms 18:10) — וְאַרְכִּין, and similar to it is 'Though he fall, he is not cast down' (ibid. 37:24): if he inclines toward the ground he does not reach the earth. But the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 24:64) is not that she dropped herself from the camel to the ground, for if so she is literally falling from upon the camel, not merely inclining from upon it, and so every expression of 'lowering' (הַרְכָּנָה) means inclining to one side only. Rather, his intent is that from upon the camel on which she was riding she inclined herself to one side, to turn her face away from him. And similar to it, in my opinion, is 'And Naaman saw him running after him, and he fell from the chariot to meet him, and he said, Is it well?' (2 Kings 5:21), whose meaning is only that he lowered himself in the chariot toward the runner who was on the ground to inquire after his welfare. And so it says there (ibid. v. 26), 'As a man turns from upon his chariot to meet you.' Or, according to Onkelos, 'מֵעַל הַגָּמָל' (from upon the camel) may mean 'עַל הַגָּמָל' (upon the camel), as in 'Your steadfastness is מֵעַל שָׁמַיִם (above the heavens)' (Psalms 108:5), and the mem of this type is like 'From there shall no more come an infant of days or an old man' (Isaiah 65:20), and likewise 'The waters that are above the heavens' (Psalms 148:4). And according to the plain sense it is like 'And behold, a great people going from the road after him' (2 Samuel 13:34), and so there are many."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "Ramban refers to Ibn Ezra as 'Rabbi Abraham.' The phrase 'turned aside from the road' renders יָרַט הַדֶּרֶךְ, an unusual word; the translation follows the contextual sense.", 1 "The verse citations from Psalms 37:24 and 108:5 are inferred from the Hebrew phrases quoted; the chapter and verse numbers follow standard enumeration."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:47.679663320Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}